1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,052
Lesson 10.8.

2
00:00:02,432 --> 00:00:05,008
Energy metrics for LLMs.

3
00:00:06,117 --> 00:00:09,277
An energy ratio is only as
good as its denominator.

4
00:00:09,657 --> 00:00:13,099
One hundred times more efficient:
against which GPU, at what

5
00:00:13,099 --> 00:00:16,943
utilization, counting which parts
of the model and at what quality?

6
00:00:17,323 --> 00:00:20,608
Today we learn the metrics
and apply them to the Z1T

7
00:00:20,608 --> 00:00:23,894
figures, to separate the
headline from the net gain.

8
00:00:25,017 --> 00:00:25,906
Three metrics.

9
00:00:26,286 --> 00:00:29,432
Joules per token: the energy
to generate one token.

10
00:00:29,812 --> 00:00:32,113
Joules per operation: the cost of a

11
00:00:32,113 --> 00:00:35,072
floating-point operation
at peak performance.

12
00:00:35,452 --> 00:00:40,247
And utilization, MFU, the fraction
of the peak that is actually used.

13
00:00:40,627 --> 00:00:45,018
They are related like this: energy
per token is twice the parameters,

14
00:00:45,018 --> 00:00:48,774
times the energy per operation,
divided by the utilization.

15
00:00:49,154 --> 00:00:56,006
With 11.55 million parameters and 0.177
picojoules per operation, we get 4.09

16
00:00:56,006 --> 00:01:02,859
microjoules at one hundred percent,
exactly the H100 figure in the Z1T table.

17
00:01:03,983 --> 00:01:06,804
The Z1T headline, one hundred thirty-nine

18
00:01:06,804 --> 00:01:09,625
times, uses a utilization of ten percent.

19
00:01:10,005 --> 00:01:14,717
It is reasonable for batch-one decode,
but the article itself mentions that

20
00:01:14,717 --> 00:01:18,674
in models like Llama three
utilization is around forty percent.

21
00:01:19,054 --> 00:01:22,604
With that value, the advantage
drops to about thirty-five times.

22
00:01:22,984 --> 00:01:26,246
No number is false; what
changes is the assumption.

23
00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:28,429
Now the scope.

24
00:01:28,809 --> 00:01:31,441
The headline excludes the vocabulary head.

25
00:01:31,821 --> 00:01:35,323
Let's compare with the same
scope on both sides, assuming a

26
00:01:35,323 --> 00:01:38,885
vocabulary of fifty thousand
two hundred fifty-seven tokens,

27
00:01:38,885 --> 00:01:41,972
GPT-2's, because the article
does not give the size.

28
00:01:42,352 --> 00:01:46,063
On the H100, the head costs
9.1 microjoules at one

29
00:01:46,063 --> 00:01:49,478
hundred percent, or
ninety-one at ten percent.

30
00:01:49,858 --> 00:01:53,809
On Z1T, on the FPGA, about
one hundred thirty-six.

31
00:01:54,189 --> 00:01:57,828
With everything included, at
ten percent there is a tie, and

32
00:01:57,828 --> 00:02:00,981
at forty percent the GPU
is about four times better.

33
00:02:01,361 --> 00:02:04,595
This is our own estimate,
with an explicit assumption.

34
00:02:05,717 --> 00:02:06,674
Quality is missing.

35
00:02:07,054 --> 00:02:10,511
A sparse model of equal size
is worse than a dense one.

36
00:02:10,891 --> 00:02:15,740
According to the article, matching the
loss of GPT-2 small requires on the

37
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:20,328
order of ten times more training
operations: that is the sparsity tax.

38
00:02:20,708 --> 00:02:24,496
At inference, an equal-quality
comparison could require a

39
00:02:24,496 --> 00:02:28,019
larger sparse model, and
that factor is not reported.

40
00:02:28,399 --> 00:02:32,254
The net gain is the headline
divided by all these factors.

41
00:02:33,383 --> 00:02:37,141
A fair comparison declares
five things: the assumed GPU

42
00:02:37,141 --> 00:02:41,309
utilization, the scope of what
is counted, including the head

43
00:02:41,309 --> 00:02:44,861
and data movement, the
quality at which it compares,

44
00:02:45,241 --> 00:02:48,600
the ratio of the full system
and that of the thermodynamic

45
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:51,610
fabric alone, and the
evidence label of each number.

46
00:02:51,990 --> 00:02:56,530
With that, the lesson of Z1T is clear:
the thermodynamic layers are very

47
00:02:56,530 --> 00:03:01,195
efficient, and the pending work is
bringing everything else to that level.

48
00:03:02,317 --> 00:03:06,212
When writing an energy claim:
report joules per token with

49
00:03:06,212 --> 00:03:10,174
utilization, batch and scope;
give the system ratio and the

50
00:03:10,174 --> 00:03:14,136
fabric ratio; and declare the
quality and the sparsity tax.

51
00:03:14,516 --> 00:03:18,611
In lab twenty-nine you will build
your headline-versus-net table.

52
00:03:19,717 --> 00:03:22,769
Common mistakes: comparing
with a different scope.

53
00:03:23,149 --> 00:03:26,582
Choosing the GPU utilization
that maximizes the ratio.

54
00:03:26,962 --> 00:03:28,998
Comparing models of different quality.

55
00:03:29,378 --> 00:03:32,538
And mixing measured and
projected numbers without labels.

56
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:39,227
In summary, energy per token is twice the
parameters times the energy per operation,

57
00:03:39,227 --> 00:03:44,074
divided by the utilization, and with
it we reconstructed the H100 figure.

58
00:03:44,454 --> 00:03:50,077
The Z1T headline depends on ten percent
utilization and excludes the head; with the

59
00:03:50,077 --> 00:03:55,023
same scope and our vocabulary assumption,
there is a tie or the GPU wins.

60
00:03:55,402 --> 00:03:59,629
The net gain requires declaring
utilization, scope and quality.

61
00:04:00,750 --> 00:04:05,129
To practice: compute the energy
per token at twenty-five percent.

62
00:04:05,509 --> 00:04:07,663
Compute the ratio at forty percent.

63
00:04:08,043 --> 00:04:11,111
Compute the head operations
with another vocabulary.

64
00:04:11,491 --> 00:04:14,561
And list the five items
of a fair comparison.
